# Copy to .env and fill in.  .env is gitignored; never commit real keys.
#
# LOCAL STACK - `supabase start` prints these. They are the same well-known
# development keys on every machine, so they are safe to share.
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<paste the anon key printed by `supabase start`>

# REMOTE PROJECT - Dashboard > Project Settings > API
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<anon public key>

# Never put the service_role key in a client-side app. It bypasses RLS.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# TASK-304 - SSO providers. Tourist sign-in is Google/Apple only; email and
# password signup stays disabled. config.toml reads these via env().
# The remote project needs the SAME values set in
# Dashboard > Authentication > Providers - config.toml configures LOCAL only.
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_APPLE_CLIENT_ID=
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_APPLE_SECRET=

# TASK-400 - OPTIONAL. An existing CMS admin account (a row in public.app_admins).
# `npm run test:db` uses these to exercise the admin half of the storage
# policies - audio_tracks_admin_read_all, which is what lets the CMS handle
# unpublished audio. Skipped when unset, so a fresh clone still runs green.
#
# EMAIL AUTH POSTURE - two switches, and only one of them lives in this repo.
#
#   [auth.email] enable_signup = false   in supabase/config.toml
#       Closes email/password SIGNUP. This is the TASK-302 decision: tourists
#       arrive through Google/Apple SSO and nobody self-registers. It governs
#       the LOCAL stack only.
#
#   "Enable Email provider"              Dashboard > Authentication > Providers
#       Governs email/password SIGN-IN. Must be ON for the two variables below
#       to work at all. It is a per-project Dashboard setting, so `db push`
#       cannot carry it and config.toml does not describe it.
#
# Those are independent. The remote project had the PROVIDER off until
# 2026-08-29, which made every admin sign-in fail with "Email logins are
# disabled" even though the account existed and the password was right - the
# error names the provider, not the credentials. Enabled there now, with signup
# still closed, which is the intended posture: no self-registration, but an
# existing CMS admin can authenticate.
# SUPABASE_ADMIN_EMAIL=
# SUPABASE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# TASK-401/402 - media pipeline. The processing service shells out to ffmpeg and
# ffprobe. Resolution order is this variable, then PATH - nothing else, so the
# build that runs is always the build you named. `npm run media:doctor` prints
# which one resolved and whether it has loudnorm and the aac encoder.
# FFMPEG_PATH=/usr/bin/ffmpeg
# FFPROBE_PATH=/usr/bin/ffprobe

# TASK-500 - OPTIONAL. Pin `npm run test:db` to one tour.
# Unset, the script verifies the first PUBLISHED tour the anon key can see,
# which is correct on both environments without editing it:
#   local  -> supabase/seed.sql inserts aaaaaaaa-0000-4000-8000-000000000001
#   remote -> prod_test_seed.sql inserts eeeeeeee-0000-4000-8000-000000000001
# Note that `supabase db push` does NOT apply supabase/seed.sql - [db.seed] runs
# on a local `db reset` only, so the local id does not exist remotely.
# TEST_TOUR_ID=

# TASK-508 - REQUIRED for `npm run seed:telaviv` (the Tel Aviv Field QA tour).
# Same admin account as TASK-400 above; the seed script drives the real CMS API,
# so every cms_* call needs a session that resolves to a row in app_admins.
# Without these the script refuses to start, before doing any work.
# The script also needs ffmpeg AND ffprobe (see FFMPEG_PATH above) - it pushes
# the generated speech through the same -16 LUFS pipeline as real narration.
#   npm run seed:telaviv -- --plan       route geometry only, no deps at all
#   npm run seed:telaviv -- --tts-only <dir>   Hebrew MP3s to listen to, network only
#   npm run seed:telaviv -- --dry-run    adds the media pipeline; needs ffmpeg

# TASK-701 - routing (Valhalla). Server-side only: never ship the key in the app.
# VALHALLA_ROUTE_URL defaults to Stadia Maps (https://api.stadiamaps.com/route/v1),
# which needs STADIA_API_KEY. A self-hosted Valhalla (e.g. http://localhost:8002/route)
# needs no key. `npm run test:routing` uses neither - the provider is stubbed.
# VALHALLA_ROUTE_URL=
# STADIA_API_KEY=
